<h2>Direct Answer</h2>
<p>A steel warehouse cost cannot be reduced to a fixed price per square metre. The two largest variables are <strong>steel tonnage</strong> (set by span, eave height, crane loads, wind and seismic loads) and <strong>roof and wall cladding</strong> (set by area, panel type, insulation and openings). A 20 m clear-span warehouse with 6 m eaves and no crane uses far less steel than a 30 m span building with 10 m eaves and a 10-tonne overhead crane — even when the floor area is identical.</p>
<p>To price a project accurately you need five things: dimensions, design loads, crane requirements, cladding specification, and destination (for transport). Everything below explains how each of those moves the number.</p>

<div class="table-scroll"><table class="cost-share">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Cost driver</th><th>What moves it</th><th>Indicative share of total</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Main steel frame (columns, rafters, bracing)</td><td>Span, height, loads, crane</td><td>40–60%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roof and wall cladding</td><td>Area, panel type, insulation</td><td>15–30%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Accessories (doors, windows, skylights, gutters)</td><td>Opening count and size</td><td>5–15%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Surface treatment</td><td>Coating system, corrosion environment</td><td>3–10%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Engineering and drawings</td><td>Design complexity, code requirements</td><td>2–8%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Packing and transport</td><td>Destination, container optimisation</td><td>5–15%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="table-note">Ranges are indicative for typical portal-frame warehouses. Real proportions shift with specification and destination.</p>

<h2>Why "Price per Square Metre" Is Misleading</h2>
<p>Two warehouses with the same floor area can carry very different steel weights. A 20 × 50 m building at 6 m eaves with no crane may need roughly <strong>25–35 kg of steel per m²</strong>. The same footprint at 10 m eaves with a 30 m clear span and a 10-tonne overhead crane can need <strong>50–70 kg per m²</strong> — close to double.</p>
<p>The per-m² figure also hides:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cladding area</strong> — a taller building has more wall area even when the floor is unchanged.</li>
  <li><strong>Crane runway beams</strong> — extra steel along the crane path, invisible in floor area.</li>
  <li><strong>Insulation</strong> — a cold-storage warehouse needs thicker insulated panels than a dry-goods shed.</li>
  <li><strong>Local design loads</strong> — high-wind or seismic sites need heavier members than a moderate climate.</li>
</ul>
<p>When you compare quotations, ask for <strong>steel tonnage</strong>, <strong>cladding area</strong> and an <strong>itemised accessory list</strong> — not just a total.</p>

<h2>Steel Tonnage: The Single Biggest Cost Factor</h2>
<p>The main frame — columns, rafters, bracing and crane runway beams where applicable — is usually the largest line item. Tonnage is driven by:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Span</strong>: wider spans need deeper, heavier rafters. Moving from 20 m to 30 m clear span can increase frame weight by 30–50%.</li>
  <li><strong>Eave height</strong>: taller columns add member weight and may require heavier bracing.</li>
  <li><strong>Crane loads</strong>: a 5-tonne crane adds far less steel than a 20-tonne crane.</li>
  <li><strong>Wind and seismic loads</strong>: higher design loads mean thicker plates and larger sections.</li>
  <li><strong>Roof slope</strong>: a steeper roof lengthens rafters and adds weight.</li>
  <li><strong>Bay spacing</strong>: wider bays (9 m vs 6 m) cut the column count but increase purlin and rafter sizes.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
  <img src="/images/projects/shenzhen-qianhai-dreamfactory-construction-site-1.jpg" alt="Steel frame columns, rafters and bracing during erection" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
  <figcaption>Steel frame during erection — the weight of columns, rafters and bracing is the primary cost driver.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Check whether each supplier states its <strong>assumed design loads</strong>. If two suppliers assume different wind speeds or seismic zones, their tonnages differ and the totals are not comparable.</p>

<h2>Roof and Wall Cladding</h2>
<p>Cladding is the second major cost block:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Panel type</strong>: single-skin sheeting is the cheapest. Insulated sandwich panels (EPS, rock wool or PU) cost more but add thermal and acoustic performance.</li>
  <li><strong>Insulation thickness</strong>: 50 mm is standard for mild climates; 75–100 mm or more for cold storage and high-temperature uses.</li>
  <li><strong>Roof shape</strong>: a curved or complex roof costs more than a standard dual-pitch roof.</li>
  <li><strong>Openings</strong>: roller shutters, personnel doors, windows and skylights are priced per unit — ten roller doors cost more than two.</li>
  <li><strong>Gutters and downpipes</strong>: required on every building; size follows local rainfall intensity.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
  <img src="/images/projects/futian-bonded-zone-customs-street-view.jpg" alt="Finished logistics warehouse exterior with wall cladding and roller doors" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
  <figcaption>Finished warehouse exterior — panel type, insulation and door count directly affect cost.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>If you compare quotations, confirm whether the cladding price includes <strong>insulation</strong>, <strong>flashings</strong>, <strong>gutters</strong> and <strong>fixings</strong>. Some suppliers quote panels only and add accessories separately.</p>

<h2>Crane and Mezzanine Impact</h2>
<p>An overhead crane is one of the largest single cost adders. It changes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Column design</strong>: crane columns must resist lateral and vertical crane loads, so sections get heavier.</li>
  <li><strong>Runway beams</strong>: additional steel members along the crane path.</li>
  <li><strong>Bracing</strong>: more horizontal bracing to resist crane surge.</li>
  <li><strong>Foundation loads</strong>: higher column reactions mean larger footings — a local cost, but part of the project budget.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
  <img src="/images/products/detail-overhead-crane-warehouse.jpg" alt="Overhead crane and runway beams inside a steel warehouse" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" />
  <figcaption>Crane capacity and lifting height drive steel frame weight and cost.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>A mezzanine floor adds a secondary steel structure — columns, beams, decking — and can raise total steel weight by 20–40% depending on floor area and load rating. Specify the <strong>floor load rating</strong> (for example 500 kg/m² or 1000 kg/m²) because it decides member sizes.</p>

<h2>Design Loads and Code Requirements</h2>
<p>The structure must satisfy the local building code. Standards we work to include <strong>GB 50017</strong> (China), <strong>AISC 360</strong> (United States), <strong>EN 1993 / Eurocode 3</strong> (Europe) and <strong>AS 4100</strong> (Australia).</p>
<p>Key load inputs that decide member sizes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Basic wind velocity at the site</li>
  <li>Seismic zone and importance factor</li>
  <li>Snow load (cold climates)</li>
  <li>Crane capacity, classification and lifting height</li>
  <li>Roof live load for maintenance or equipment</li>
</ul>
<p>If you source from China for a project elsewhere, confirm the supplier can design to <strong>your local code</strong> and that drawings will be accepted by your authority. Many buyers appoint a locally registered engineer to review or stamp fabrication drawings.</p>

<h2>Steel Grade, Surface Treatment and Corrosion Protection</h2>
<p>Grade selection affects both price per tonne and total weight. High-strength grades cost more per tonne but can cut weight through a lighter design; standard grades such as Q235B and Q355B are usually the cost-effective choice for warehouses. Confirm the grade against your design requirements and local code rather than by price alone.</p>
<p>Surface treatment protects the steel and sets maintenance cost:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Shot blasting</strong>: preparation before coating, typically Sa 2.5.</li>
  <li><strong>Primer plus topcoat</strong>: standard two-coat system for moderate environments.</li>
  <li><strong>Hot-dip galvanising</strong>: higher cost, far better resistance for humid or coastal sites — often 5–15% of the steel cost.</li>
  <li><strong>Fire-resistant coating</strong>: required by some codes for certain occupancies.</li>
</ul>
<p>A dry inland warehouse may need only primer and topcoat; a coastal site or one storing corrosive materials usually needs galvanising or a higher-specification system.</p>

<h2>Shipping, Complexity and Quotation Terms</h2>
<p>For export projects the landed cost includes more than the structure:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Ocean freight moves with destination, season and market conditions.</li>
  <li>Container count follows building size and component dimensions — packing optimisation is real money.</li>
  <li>Inland transport at origin and destination is separate.</li>
  <li>Duties, taxes and clearance fees depend on the destination country.</li>
</ul>
<p>Confirm whether the price is <strong>FOB</strong> (factory or port), <strong>CIF</strong> (destination port) or <strong>DDP</strong> (delivered to site) before comparing totals.</p>
<p>Custom requirements also add engineering and fabrication cost: multiple roof slopes or complex geometry, mezzanine and office areas, special door configurations, non-standard column spacing, and architectural facade requirements.</p>

<h2>What to Provide for an Accurate Quotation</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Dimensions</strong>: length, width, eave height, roof pitch</li>
  <li><strong>Crane requirements</strong>: yes or no, capacity in tonnes, lifting height, number of cranes</li>
  <li><strong>Mezzanine</strong>: yes or no, floor area, load rating</li>
  <li><strong>Cladding</strong>: single-skin or insulated panel, insulation thickness, colour</li>
  <li><strong>Openings</strong>: number and size of roller doors, personnel doors, windows, skylights</li>
  <li><strong>Design loads</strong>: wind speed, seismic zone, snow load where known</li>
  <li><strong>Design code</strong>: AISC, Eurocode, GB or AS</li>
  <li><strong>Surface treatment</strong>: coating preference or corrosion environment</li>
  <li><strong>Destination</strong>: delivery port or site for transport calculation</li>
  <li><strong>Drawings or sketches</strong>: even a rough layout helps confirm bay spacing</li>
</ul>
<p>A supplier that quotes a detailed number without reviewing these items is guessing. Providing them upfront is also the cheapest way to avoid a price revision later.</p>

<h2>Common Cost Mistakes</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Comparing totals without checking tonnage</strong> — a lower total may mean lighter steel or omitted accessories.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring transport</strong> — sea freight plus inland transport can add 10–25% to delivered cost, more for remote sites.</li>
  <li><strong>Underestimating crane impact</strong> — adding a crane after quotation significantly increases frame weight.</li>
  <li><strong>Not specifying insulation</strong> — a single-skin quote is far below an insulated-panel quote.</li>
  <li><strong>Omitting doors and windows</strong> — often quoted as optional extras.</li>
  <li><strong>Assuming foundations are included</strong> — most fabricators exclude foundations; they are local civil works.</li>
  <li><strong>Not accounting for local code approval</strong> — drawings that cannot be approved locally force a redesign.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Responsibility Boundary</h2>
<p>Cost confusion usually starts at the scope boundary. This is the split we work to unless a written agreement says otherwise:</p>

<div class="table-scroll"><table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Scope</th><th>ZhongSai typically provides</th><th>Local team typically responsible</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Structural design and detailing</td><td>Engineering coordination, shop drawings, connection details</td><td>Local registered engineer review or stamping, foundation design</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fabrication</td><td>Columns, beams, purlins, cladding, accessories</td><td>—</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Quality control</td><td>Dimensional inspection, coating thickness, packing inspection</td><td>—</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Packing and loading</td><td>Component marking, packing, container loading</td><td>—</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Export and transport</td><td>Export documentation, sea freight coordination</td><td>Customs clearance, inland transport to site</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Foundation and civil works</td><td>Technical guidance on anchor bolt positions</td><td>Foundation construction, permits, authority submissions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Erection</td><td>Installation technical guidance, drawing support</td><td>Local labour, physical erection, lifting equipment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Roof and wall installation</td><td>Supply of panels and fixings</td><td>Local installation team</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="table-note">Unless the contract explicitly includes installation, the supplier's scope ends at delivery to site or at technical guidance during erection.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>How much does a steel structure warehouse cost per square metre?</h3>
<p>There is no universal per-m² price, because steel weight varies with span, height, crane loads and design loads. A simple 20 m span warehouse at 6 m eaves without a crane may use 25–35 kg of steel per m², while a 30 m span building with a 10-tonne crane can use 50–70 kg/m². The only reliable comparison is an itemised quotation with tonnage, cladding area and accessory list.</p>

<h3>What is the cheapest type of steel warehouse?</h3>
<p>A single-span portal frame with moderate eave height (5–6 m), no crane, single-skin roof and wall cladding, few openings and standard surface treatment. Cranes, mezzanines, insulated panels and complex geometry all add cost — and the cheapest option may not meet your operating needs.</p>

<h3>Does the steel supplier include the foundation?</h3>
<p>Most fabricators do not include foundation construction. Foundations are designed by a locally registered engineer from soil conditions and local codes, and built by a local civil contractor. The supplier typically provides anchor bolt positions and technical guidance.</p>

<h3>How can I reduce cost without compromising safety?</h3>
<p>Work on variables that reduce steel weight: optimise span, avoid unnecessary eave height, use standard bay spacing, and choose single-skin cladding where insulation is not needed. Never reduce design loads below code requirements — that is a safety risk and may fail local approval.</p>

<h3>How long does an accurate quotation take?</h3>
<p>With complete dimensions, loads and specifications, a detailed quotation usually takes 3–7 working days. With only rough dimensions you get an indicative estimate that must be revised once drawings and load data are confirmed.</p>

<h3>What should I check when comparing two quotations?</h3>
<p>Compare total tonnage and unit price, cladding area and panel type, the accessory list, surface treatment specification, whether engineering and drawings are included, packing and transport terms, and the assumed design loads and code. A lower total may reflect lighter steel, omitted accessories or different load assumptions.</p>

<h3>What payment terms are common?</h3>
<p>Typically a deposit on order confirmation with the balance before shipment. Terms vary with supplier, project size and negotiation, and should be written into the contract before production starts.</p>

